lly address sexuality and relationships after amputation. There are peer mentors who have been through exactly this and can talk to you man-to-man about the things that feel too awkward to ask a doctor."\n\n**6. Connect to peer support.**\n"Speaking of which — there is a veteran in our peer support program who lost both legs in Afghanistan 8 years ago. He is married now, coaches his kid\'s soccer team from his chair. He has specifically offered to talk with new bilateral amputees. No pressure, but I think one conversation with him might be worth more than anything I can say. Would you be open to that?"\n\n**7. Address the bigger existential crisis.**\n"I also heard you say last week that you don\'t see the point anymore. I want to check in on that. When you say that, are you telling me you are having thoughts of hurting yourself, or are you telling me you feel lost and do not know what your life looks like now?"\n[Assess suicide risk directly based on response]\n\n**8. Offer hope grounded in reality.**\n"Your life at 25 is going to look very different from what you imagined at 18. That is true. But different does not mean less. The next year is going to be the hardest year of your life — prosthetic training, adapting, grieving. But you are 20 years old, you are strong, and you have resources that most people in this situation do not have. I am not asking you to be optimistic right now. I am asking you to stay in the fight long enough to see what comes next."\n\n**Key Principles**: Did not dismiss his grief or offer toxic positivity. Validated the relationship loss as real. Challenged hopeless thinking with concrete examples. Addressed intimacy directly without awkwardness. Offered peer mentoring as the most powerful intervention. Assessed suicide risk when the opening presented. Communicated hope without demanding positivity.',
